Create Linux Containers and Kubernetes Pods
Objectives
Run containers inside pods and identify the host OS processes and namespaces that the containers use.

1. Running Containers in Pods
In Kubernetes and Red Hat OpenShift, applications run inside containers, and containers run inside pods.
•	Container Image → Immutable package containing application + dependencies.
•	Pod → Smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes.
Example flow:
Container Image → Container → Pod → Node
To create a pod quickly (for testing/debugging), use:
oc run RESOURCE/NAME --image IMAGE
Example:
oc run web-server --image registry.access.redhat.com/ubi10/httpd-24
Explanation:
Part	Meaning
oc	OpenShift CLI
run	create pod
web-server	pod name
--image	container image
This command creates a pod with one container running Apache HTTP server.
⚠️ For production, use YAML manifests like Deployments instead of oc run.
________________________________________
2. Running Custom Commands in Containers
Normally, a container runs the default command from the image.
But you can override it using:
--command --
Syntax:
oc run NAME --image IMAGE --command -- cmd arg1 arg2
Example:
oc run my-app --image registry.access.redhat.com/ubi10/ubi \
--command -- date
Result → Container runs date command instead of default program.
________________________________________
3. Passing Arguments to Default Command
If you want to keep default command but add arguments, use:
oc run NAME --image IMAGE -- arg1 arg2
Example:
oc run app --image nginx -- -g "daemon off;"
Here:
•	nginx command stays default
•	arguments are added.
________________________________________
4. Interactive Container Shell
To open terminal inside container:
oc run -it my-app --image registry.access.redhat.com/ubi10/ubi \
--command -- /bin/bash
Options explanation:
Option	Meaning
-i	keep stdin open
-t	allocate terminal
-it	interactive shell
Output:
bash-5.1$
Now you are inside the container shell.
________________________________________
5. Pod Restart Policy
Restart policy decides what happens if container stops.
Command:
--restart POLICY
Policies:
Policy	Meaning
Always	restart every time (default)
OnFailure	restart only if error
Never	never restart
Example:
oc run my-app --image ubi --restart Never --command -- date
Here pod runs date once and stops.
________________________________________
6. Automatically Delete Pod
To remove pod automatically after execution:
--rm
Example:
oc run -it my-app --rm --image ubi --restart Never --command -- date
Result:
pod deleted after execution
Useful for temporary debugging containers.
________________________________________
7. Setting Environment Variables
Some applications require environment variables.
Command:
--env KEY=VALUE
Example:
oc run mysql --image registry.redhat.io/rhel9/mysql-80 \
--env MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=myP@$$123
This sets MySQL root password.
________________________________________
8. User IDs and Security in OpenShift
OpenShift uses random UID assignment for security.
Check project UID range:
oc describe project my-app
Example output:
uid-range=1000710000/10000
Meaning:
Value	Meaning
1000710000	starting UID
10000	size of range
So containers get UID between:
1000710000 → 1000719999
Important rules:
•	Containers run as non-root user
•	Primary group = root group (GID 0)
•	Files must have group write permission
Why?
Security.
If container runs as root:
•	Can modify host OS
•	Can install software
•	Can delete system files
So OpenShift enforces rootless containers.
________________________________________
9. Pod Security
Kubernetes uses Pod Security Admission (PSA).
Profiles:
Profile	Security level
privileged	full permissions
baseline	moderate security
restricted	most secure
OpenShift also uses SCC (Security Context Constraints) to enforce security.
Security Context controls:
•	user ID
•	capabilities
•	privileges
________________________________________
10. Execute Command Inside Running Container
To run a command in a running pod:
oc exec POD_NAME -- COMMAND
Example:
oc exec my-app -- date
Output:
Tue Feb 21 20:43:53 UTC
________________________________________
For Multi-Container Pods
Specify container:
oc exec POD -c CONTAINER -- COMMAND
Example:
oc exec my-app -c ruby-container -- date
________________________________________
Interactive Terminal Inside Running Container
oc exec my-app -it -- bash
Options:
Option	Meaning
-i	interactive
-t	terminal
Exit:
exit
________________________________________
11. Container Logs
Containers send logs to:
stdout
stderr
Retrieve logs:
oc logs POD_NAME
Example:
oc logs postgresql-1-jw89j
________________________________________
Important options:
Option	Meaning
--tail=10	last 10 log lines
-f	follow logs (live streaming)
-c container	logs of specific container
-p	previous container logs
Example:
oc logs postgresql --tail=10
________________________________________
12. Attach to Running Container
Attach terminal to running container:
oc attach POD_NAME -it
Example:
oc attach my-app -it
This connects to container’s terminal.
________________________________________
13. Deleting Kubernetes Resources
Delete pod:
oc delete pod POD_NAME
Example:
oc delete pod php-app
________________________________________
Delete using label:
oc delete pod -l app=my-app
Delete using YAML file:
oc delete -f file.yaml
________________________________________
14. Pod Graceful Termination
Pods shutdown gracefully.
Default behaviour:
1.	Pod receives SIGTERM
2.	Waits for process to stop
3.	Then kills container.
Change time:
oc delete pod php-app --grace-period=10
Force immediate delete:
oc delete pod php-app --now
Force delete (dangerous):
oc delete pod php-app --force
⚠️ May cause data loss.
________________________________________
15. CRI-O Container Engine
OpenShift uses CRI-O as container runtime.
Container runtime responsibilities:
•	run containers
•	manage images
•	manage container processes
Unlike Docker/Podman, CRI-O is built specifically for Kubernetes.
________________________________________
16. crictl Command
crictl is CLI for container runtime.
Common commands:
Command	Purpose
crictl pods	list pods
crictl ps	running containers
crictl images	images
crictl inspect	container details
crictl logs	container logs
crictl exec	run command
________________________________________
17. Find Pod Node
First identify node where pod runs:
oc get pods -o wide
Example output:
postgresql-1  Running  master01
Get node name:
oc get pod POD_NAME -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeName}'
________________________________________
18. Debug Node
Cluster admins can debug nodes:
oc debug node/master01
Then access host binaries:
chroot /host
Now host tools are accessible.
________________________________________
19. Find Container ID
crictl ps --name postgresql
Example:
CONTAINER ID   NAME
27943ae4f3024  postgresql
________________________________________
20. Get Container PID
crictl inspect CONTAINER_ID
Example:
crictl inspect 27943ae4f3024 | grep pid
Output:
pid: 43453
________________________________________
21. List Container Namespaces
Use Linux command:
lsns -p PID
Example:
lsns -p 43453
Shows namespaces:
•	PID namespace
•	network namespace
•	mount namespace
•	IPC namespace
•	UTS namespace
These isolate container from host.
________________________________________
22. Enter Container Namespace
Use:
nsenter
Example:
nsenter -t 43453 -p -r ps -ef
Options:
Option	Meaning
-t	target PID
-p	process namespace
-r	root filesystem
Execute in all namespaces:
nsenter -t 43453 -a ps -ef
